 "display": {
  "transcript_display": "show_with_label",
  "title": "Episode 260: Asarah B'Teves and Waiting for Moshiach",
  "title_basis": "content",
  "summary_short": "Contest runners-up are announced, a first-grade class sings to celebrate their first siddur, and Uncle Yossi explains Asarah B'Teves: the siege of Yerushalayim, and why we cry out 'Ad Masai'.",
  "summary_long": "Broadcast two days after Asarah B'Teves 5747. Uncle Yossi reads a long list of runners-up in the My Shabbos Candlestick contest, whose prizes wait at the Tzivos Hashem gift shop, and leads the Hakochainik march. A first-grade class from a Boro Park girls' school, which received its first siddurim from Uncle Yossi, sings a song saying goodbye to the old Alef-Beis book and hello to the new siddur. Two young siblings visiting from Ann Arbor, Michigan, lead the Shema. Then Uncle Yossi turns to the Jewish calendar: as Shlomo HaMelech said, there is a time for everything, even a time to be sad in order to grow. On the tenth of Teves, Nevuchadnetzar's army besieged Yerushalayim, leading to the breach of the walls on the seventeenth of Tammuz and the destruction of the Beis HaMikdash on Tisha B'Av. As long as we learn Torah and keep mitzvos, Hashem, the Guardian of Israel, protects us, and He promised to turn these fast days into Yomim Tovim when Moshiach comes. The show ends with everyone calling out 'Ad Masai' and a sponsor message.",
  "lesson": "We remember sad days in order to improve ourselves, and through Torah and mitzvos we bring Moshiach, when these fasts will become days of joy.",
